hat tube to do the next steps of  your experiment  and that's where the final movie at the  bottom comes in and this is something  called a flow dropometer  we call it a flow dropometer or  fluorescence activated droplet sorter  and what it is is basically the droplet  microfluidic analog to a float  to a fluorescence activated cell sorter  um  we take our droplets that have been  formed you know by our previous  instrument  we pack them together and flow them into  this device  that scans them with a laser and then  sorts them according to  the fluorescence measurement uh that's  picked up by the detector  and it's built basically like a flow  cytometer at least in terms of the  analysis part  you have a multi-color detector with  filters and so on that are capturing the  emitted fluorescent light  and then that's being analyzed in real  time by a computer to determine whether  to sort a droplet  the sorting is based on a different  force from typical facts machine  it's uh uses dielectrophoresis and  that's really enabled  by the fact that we have an emulsion we  have a a conductive droplet  uh in an immiscible non-conductive oil  and that um difference in in the um  the the electrical properties of those  fluids the conductive versus the  non-conductive carrier phase  allows us to take advantage of  dielectrophoresis to apply very large  forces to these very very tiny droplets  and actually do deflections as you can  see  in this movie to sort  um so these are kind of the complete  tool kit of droplet microfluidics and  when you conceive of a new idea  typically you take these components and  you mix and match them together  and to optimize for whatever you're  trying to accomplish and what i'm going  to tell you through the rest of this  talk  is a few specific applications where  we've taken  these modules and we've built specific  instruments to perform  a measurement that we thought was  important  so the first thing i'm going to tell you  about is  single cell dna sequencing and this  really gets the bigger theme of my talk  which is on single cell multi-omics  so um omics you know this is a  term that's used to describe when you  want to perform a a  kind of a broad profiling of a universal  kind of information so the  typical olympics is genomics which we've  all probably heard of before  where you want to characterize the  entire genome of an organism let's say  or  the the collection of genomes coming out  of a microbial ecosystem where you could  have  literally millions of distinct cells in  just a small you know  a teaspoon full of seawater for example  now multi-omics basically describes  doing  multiple kinds of omics simultaneously  and and that's what my  the primary idea of my talk is single  cell dna sequencing would be kind of the  base  omics that we're going to perform and  the reason that you would want to  perform this  in the case of let's say microbes is  that if we look at the microbial world  you know there's a huge diversity of  organisms um  and and even just you know a  relatively boring sample like a piece of  crud on the bottom of your shoe could  have  thousands of different species of  bacteria and yeast and so on all mixed  together  um and uh you know we we are also  ecosystems for  for um microbes as well and you know our  gut is full of microbes that help us  digest food and sometimes can make us  sick we have microbes all over our skin  and understanding this microbial life  this diversity is actually  potentially very important to  understanding how those ecosystems  function in the case of human health  how they impact human health now the big  challenge really comes from the fact  that  the amount of dna encoding an individual  microbe is actually fairly large so uh  you know if you think about it just in  terms of information  you have on the order of roughly 10  million  nucleotides for just a very simple  microbe simple bacterium  and if you imagine having hundreds of  thousands 