ull onto that cell that  synthetic nucleic acid tag  and so this molecule this oligo function  this this um oligoconjugated antibody  is essentially kind of a transducer it  transduces a protein based signal as is  recognized by the  the antibody into a nucleic acid signal  that you can read out with your  um with your dna sequencer  and and the nice thing about this  simpler to similar to the aptamer work  is that now we can just  uh sequence that dna along with the dna  of the cell  getting tagging it just the same way  using the barcodes and we can get all  that information out simultaneously  so we built a microfluidic device to do  that and uh  it uses basically exactly the same  method that you've seen previously  and lo and behold we're able to label  different cells  with different combinations of  antibodies and get nice clusters that  look  for all intents and purposes they look  like what you get from a flow cytometer  using fluorescence  tags but these are our informatic  accounts  sequence counts to label the different  spots here  and what um some very clever labs  recognize is that well  you can actually combine ab seek in  combination with rna-seq as well and  perform multi-omics  these are called sight seek and reap  seek where they recognize that if you  just polyadenylate the  um oligo tag that we're using to label  our our  antibodies those will be captured by the  rna sequence analysis  uh that's typically used because you all  mrna is polyadenylated in eukaryotes  and so if you polyadenylate your  antibody tags those are going to get  captured by the same electron body so  now you can start to do  protein and rna and actually these are  now available through some commercial  companies  um for dna and protein it's a little  more challenging because  um uh in the dna analysis we're not  doing a poly  a capture we are actually sequencing  distinct loci of the genome that have  distinct  primers associated with them so in the  case of a cancer cell