all right well it's a pleasure to be  here to speak with you today over the  web  and tell you about what my lab is  working on  um so we're primarily a microfluidics  lab a microfluidic lab that  builds technology for doing biological  research  essentially we consider ourselves  research tools builders  and today i'm going to tell you about a  major focus in the lab  it's about what a third of my lab does  where we use  droplet-based microfluidics to perform  single cell multi-omics  so droplet-based microfluidics is a  subfield of microfluidics where the  overall concept  is to use microfluidic devices  to form these very small picoliter  volume  droplets of water that are surrounded by  an immiscible oil  phase and the microfluidic devices form  them and they also process them  and functionally the way that we think  of it is that the droplets  are the reaction containers the test  tubes if you will  and the microfluidics are the graduate  students or the robots or the pipettors  they're the  the instrumentation that is performing  the operations on those test tubes to do  the biology they will want to do  so um when when we um  generally the way the research goes is  that we conceive of an idea you know a  new  you know it'd be great to be able to  form some new measurement  and we think about what that measurement  entails and we break it down into a  bunch of different steps  and we then build a microfluidic  instrument in essence that  automates doing those different steps  and it's usually just you know what you  would typically do by hand  you know at the bench but now we we  miniaturize that into a microfluidic  chip and all of those steps are done  with full automation on the chip or as  much automation as we can get  and so shown in this slide are the basic  uh modules we call them of droplet  microfluidics  which are um you know first here at the  top left is a droplet  generator this is the microfluidic  device that generates the test tubes  it also encapsulates samples so if you  have say a suspension of cells and you  want to analyze individual cells  you use that droplet generator to  encapsulate those cells in individual  droplets where they can be further  processed  encapsulation is just usually the first  and most basic thing we need to do in  droplet microfluidics  if you want to do more complex assays on  for example those cells that you've  encapsulated  this will require you to go and often  add reagents to the droplets which we do  by pico injection which is the  movie in the middle there and then  sometimes when we want to add a large  volume  to a given droplet so let's say we  digest the cell  to open up its genome get a genome ready  for for  analysis at that point we essentially  want to do a buffer exchange and add a  new reagent  and we do that by expanding that small  concentrated droplet into a very large  droplet as shown  in that movie to the top right  um there's a bunch of other different  modules in drop of microfluidics  these are kind of the most commonly used  modules that allow you to just  form a huge number of droplets and  encapsulate  lots of cells lots of particles  molecules and so on  and to do multi-step reactions in the  droplets now  the reason that this is so powerful is  that is really the speed  and the very tiny size of these droplets  so  the the natural time scale of droplet  microfluidics is around a kilohertz so  the natural rate at which the droplets  are going to be formed and processed by  the microfluidic devices  is about a thousand droplets per second  they can be substantially higher  up to a hundred times higher than that  and it could be uh substantially lower  about 100 times lower you'll see devices  so it spans a broad range  but um the the real sweet spot where  this technology really shines  is in making a huge number of droplets  very rapidly and because those droplets  are only picoliters that's uh  you know roughly 10 10 to the minus 12  and usually these are between  uh 10 microns to 100 microns so you're  talking about 50  picoliters of volume you can make an  enormous number of droplets from just a  relatively small amount of reagents i  believe about a half mil  of sample can form about a billion 10  micron droplets so  you know if you had a way to usefully  use every one of those droplets you can  imagine  doing an enormous number of assays and  so  where droplet microfluidics has really  gone is into  application areas where you need there's  this need for very high throughput  analysis and so much of the current uh  space is focused in single cell analysis  where you have a large number of cells  that you want to analyze individually  and they're the throughput the ability  to take a suspension of  tens of thousands hundreds of thousands  of cells and isolate them in individual  droplets  and also the very small volume which  keeps the cellular  components whether it's the lysate of a  cell that you've digested or a secreted  molecule  it keeps it concentrated to that cell in  this very small droplet  that is what enables you to get a good  measurement as well you have essentially  a very high sensitivity because  even though you're starting from a very  small amount of material you know let's  say just one cell or literally one  molecule  the actual rel the actual concentration  in that droplet because the droplet is  so small  is in a range where you can actually do  a measurement you can actually do  reactions reliably  um so those components are the basic  components that allow us to make lots of  droplets  in some instances there's also a need to  sort through that huge emulsion that you  form so maybe you're  going to use those three components to  make about 10 million droplets which is  kind of typical for us  now what do you do you have 10 million  droplets in a tube  each droplet has a different cell or  different molecule and how do you get  the  the material or the the information out  of that tube to do the next steps of  your experiment  and that's where the final movie at the  bottom comes in and this is something  called a flow dropometer  we call it a flow dropometer or  fluorescence activated droplet sorter  and what it is is basically the droplet  microfluidic analog to a float  to a fluorescence activated cell sorter  um  we take our droplets that have been  formed you know by our previous  instrument  we pack them together and flow them into  this device  that scans them with a laser and then  sorts them according to  the fluorescence measurement uh that's  picked up by the detector  and it's built basically like a flow  cytometer at least in terms of the  analysis part  you have a multi-color detector with  filters and so on that are capturing the  emitted fluorescent light  and then that's being analyzed in real  time by a computer to determine whether  to sort a droplet  the sorting is based on a different  force from typical facts machine  it's uh uses dielectrophoresis and  that's really enabled  by the fact that we have an emulsion we  have a a conductive droplet  uh in an immiscible non-conductive oil  and that um difference in in the um  the the electrical properties of those  fluids the conductive versus the  non-conductive carrier phase  allows us to take advantage of  dielectrophoresis to apply very large  forces to these very very tiny droplets  and actually do deflections as you can  see  in this movie to sort  um so these are kind of the complete  tool kit of droplet microfluidics and  when you conceive of a new idea  typically you take these components and  you mix and match them together  and to optimize for whatever you're  trying to accomplish and what i'm going  to tell you through the rest of this  talk  is a few specific applications where  we've taken  these modules and we've built specific  instruments to perform  a measurement that we thought was  important  so the first thing i'm going to tell you  about is  single ce